{
  "description": "The same required negative through a record field. Records are mutable reference values like lists (spec/execution-model.md:457-460), so writing `:p.x` inside the procedure updates the caller's record and `print :q.x` reports `99`, with no diagnostic. The postfix write is the interesting part: `:p.x = 99` resolves its base `:p` as a READ before assigning (spec/execution-model.md:492-499), and here that read succeeds because `:p` is a parameter — the sibling `a-postfix-write-reads-its-base-across-the-boundary` is the same syntax over a base the boundary hides, and it is diagnosed. The two together say the postfix rule is about visibility of the BASE, not about postfix writes being restricted.",
